
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= -1 && New_Ret != -1);
</code>

Here's the explanation:
The comment "@@@ If the return value is negative, old list size is not -1" translates to "New_Ret should be greater than or equal to -1 and not equal to -1". This is equivalent to asserting "New_Ret >= -1 && New_Ret != -1". The code "Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= -1 && New_Ret != -1);" generates this assert statement.--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 ? Old_arrListCount != -1 : true);
</code>

The above assert statement checks if the return value of the `LastIndexOf` method is negative. If it is, it asserts that the old list size is not -1. Otherwise, it will not make any assertions.--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= -1 && New_Ret >= Old_arrListCount);
</code>
--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= -1);
</code>

Note: In C#, `Debug.Assert(condition);` is used to embed assertions in the code. Assertions are used to find potential bugs in a program. If the specified condition evaluates to false, the program throws an AssertionException.--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= -1 && Old_arrListCount != -1);
</code>
--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= -1);
</code>
--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret != -1);
</code>

The code above is an assertion statement based on the comment "@@@ If the return value is negative, old list size is not -1". This assertion checks if the value of `New_Ret` is not equal to -1.
```cs

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

	    // @@@ If the return value is negative, old list size is not -1 
            Debug.Assert(New_Ret != -1);
        }
    }
}

